  "worked_solution": "To find the rank of $E,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 20 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+10{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(E)=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
